{
  "id": "code-validate-binary-search-tree",
  "title": "Validate Binary Search Tree",
  "difficulty": "medium",
  "topics": ["tree", "binary search tree", "depth-first search"],
  "asked": "very_common",
  "statement": "You're given the root of a binary tree. Return `True` if it is a valid binary search tree, and `False` otherwise.\n\nIn a valid binary search tree, every value in a node's left subtree is strictly smaller than the node's value, every value in its right subtree is strictly larger, and both subtrees are valid binary search trees themselves. Equal values are not allowed. Each node is a `TreeNode` with `val`, `left` and `right`; the class is already defined for you. In the examples, a tree is written in level order with `null` for a missing child.\n\nConstraints: 1 to 10^4 nodes; -2^31 <= val <= 2^31 - 1.",
  "functionName": "is_valid_bst",
  "signature": "def is_valid_bst(root: Optional[TreeNode]) -> bool:",
  "starterCode": "def is_valid_bst(root: Optional[TreeNode]) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Return True if the tree is a valid binary search tree.\"\"\"\n    pass\n",
  "starterCodeJs": "// TreeNode is provided: new TreeNode(val, left, right) -> { val, left, right }\n\n/**\n * Return true if the tree is a valid binary search tree.\n * @param {TreeNode|null} root\n * @return {boolean}\n */\nfunction isValidBst(root) {\n\n}\n",
  "examples": [
    {"input": "root = [5, 3, 8, 1, 4]", "output": "true", "explanation": "Everything left of 5 is smaller and everything right of it is larger, and the same holds under 3."},
    {"input": "root = [5, 3, 8, 1, 6]", "output": "false", "explanation": "6 is a fine right child for 3, but it sits in 5's left subtree and is larger than 5."},
    {"input": "root = [4, 4]", "output": "false", "explanation": "Equal values aren't allowed, so a left child equal to its parent breaks the rule."}
  ],
  "visibleTestCount": 3,
